	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Raw string literals (<code>"""</code>, C# 11 and later): the quotes sit
			on their own lines, and squill lays the query out between them at the
			closing quotes&rsquo; indent, which is where C# measures a raw
			string&rsquo;s indentation from. A raw string written on one line
			gets the same layout.
		</p>
		<p>
			Everything else is left exactly as written: plain strings, verbatim
			strings (<code>@"&hellip;"</code>).
			Interpolated strings (<code>$"&hellip;"</code>,{" "}
			<code>$"""&hellip;"""</code>) never are &mdash; SQL with holes in it
			isn&rsquo;t SQL squill can parse. To have squill format a query, write
			it as a raw string.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Dapper</h2>
		<p>
			The built-in <code>csharp</code> grammar&rsquo;s default query knows
			Dapper&rsquo;s query and execute methods, generic
			(<code>QueryAsync&lt;Order&gt;</code>) or not. No query file needed:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="csharp-dapper" lang="csharp" />
		<p>
			<code>at-params</code> makes <code>@name</code> parameters pass through
			as written &mdash;
			written tight against an operator (<code>=@customerId</code>) too &mdash;
			and so does Dapper&rsquo;s list expansion, <code>in @orderIds</code>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>EF Core</h2>
		<p>
			The default query finds raw strings passed to{" "}
			<code>migrationBuilder.Sql</code> and to the methods that take a plain
			string, <code>FromSqlRaw</code> and <code>ExecuteSqlRaw</code> and
			their async forms, and <code>SqlQueryRaw&lt;T&gt;</code>.
		</p>
		<Recipe name="csharp-efcore" lang="csharp" />
		<p>
			Two things to know. <code>FromSql</code> and <code>ExecuteSql</code>{" "}
			take an interpolated string, which squill leaves alone. And the{" "}
			<code>Raw</code> methods&rsquo;{" "}
			<code>{"{0}"}</code> placeholders aren&rsquo;t SQL: squill reports
			those strings as unparsable rather than guess. Named parameters
			(<code>@cutoff</code>, bound with a <code>DbParameter</code>) need{" "}
			<code>at-params</code>, as with Dapper.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>ADO.NET</h2>
		<p>
			A raw string assigned to <code>CommandText</code> is found on any
			command, Npgsql&rsquo;s and Microsoft.Data.Sqlite&rsquo;s alike. Set
			the dialect to match the database:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="csharp-adonet" lang="csharp" />
	</section>
</Docs>
